  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 146 AM EDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nYesterday's frontal boundary has washed out completely across our \narea but we do have a brief return to easterly flow this morning. \nLuckily numerous high clouds, a steady gradient wind and an overall \nwarmer/drier boundary layer are keeping fog in check. Flow veers to \na more S/SE direction by this afternoon and highs should recover to \naround 70F outside of our far NE. Warm/moist air advection then \nsteadily increases tonight through Thu ahead of the next cold front \nheaded for our area. Expect more mid 70s on Wed and some upper 70s \npossible by Thu dependent on exact frontal timing. As previously \nmentioned, Thursday's frontal passage will feature a much better \nprefrontal LLJ and theta-e surge, enough to at least partially \noffset the incredibly dry and stable airmass that has been in place \nfor so long. Mid/upper level support is still not great this far \nsouth with far better CVA and upper jet support over Lake Superior \nand points north. Still, enough moisture advection and low level \nconvergence to generate some very light rain showers with the best \nchances near Lake Michigan Thursday afternoon. Latest guidance even \nsuggests a few sprinkles surviving into our SE Thu evening. No \nimpacts anticipated though. Removed the brief/small NBM thunder \nmention given near total lack of instability on forecast soundings. \n\nA return to less extreme warmth Fri and Sat with highs around 70F. A \nvery high level of spread still exists among all sources of \ndeterministic and ensemble guidance for the weekend into next week. \nThis uncertainty centers around an unusually southward displaced \nPacific jet streak/shortwave emanating from the data-sparse regions \noff the Mexican coast that will eventually lift northeast into our \nregion. Models struggling with this and another upper low that \ndevelops off the northwest U.S. coast. Very low confidence regarding \nforecast details Sat-Tue. Still think Halloween will be mostly dry \nfor trick-or-treat activities but NBM maintains some low chance \nPoP's and can't entirely rule it out. Much better rain chances seem \nto arrive Sunday with remarkable uncertainty regarding a potential \nsecond system around Tuesday of next week.",
